Mutation
- [N1] Node-level mutation: element=guard(b1,n3,"v1 = \"No\""); source=i2:If there is no suitable candidate available, then advertise the vacancy.; reason=The guard uses a generic variable assignment 'v1 = "No"' instead of preserving the explicit condition 'no suitable candidate available', dropping the domain-specific qualifier.
- [N2] Node-level mutation: element=guard(b2,n5,"v2 = \"No\""); source=i3:If there is still no suitable candidate available, then advertise the vacancy again.; reason=The guard abstracts the repeated condition into 'v2 = "No"', losing the original contextual phrasing and granularity required by the strict matching standard.
- [N3] Node-level mutation: element=guard(b1,c1,"v1 = \"Yes\"") -> guard(b2,c1,"v2 = \"Yes\""); source=i4:On the other hand, if there is a suitable candidate available, then proceed with selecting the appropriate applicant.; reason=Both guards map the positive candidate availability condition to generic variable checks ('v1 = "Yes"' and 'v2 = "Yes"') rather than retaining the source text's explicit criteria.
